  "preamble": "Loss of the labio-velar approximant [w]. English lost it in initial wr- and after s in sword. Most Ancient Greek dialects lost digamma ϝ [w] entirely, and Japanese lost /w/ before every vowel except a. Initial *w was dropped in Australian initial-dropping languages, e.g. Uradhi anta- < Proto-Paman *wanta- 'put' and Arrernte angke-me < *wangka- 'speak'.",
  "phoneticEffects": "Elision, Glide Loss",
  "languageExamples": [
    {
      "language": "English",
      "languageFamily": "West Germanic",
      "examples": [
        {
          "from": "Old English wrītan 'to write'",
          "to": "write /ɹaɪt/",
          "note": "Initial w was lost before r in Early Modern English."
        },
        {
          "from": "Old English sweord 'sword'",
          "to": "sword /sɔːd/",
          "note": "w lost after s in this word."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "language": "Ancient Greek",
      "languageFamily": "Hellenic",
      "examples": [
        {
          "from": "ϝοῖκος woîkos 'house' (Proto-Hellenic *wóikos)",
          "to": "οἶκος oîkos",
          "note": "Digamma [w] was lost in Attic-Ionic and most other dialects."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "language": "Japanese",
      "languageFamily": "Japonic",
      "examples": [
        {
          "from": "ゐる wiru 'to be, exist'",
          "to": "いる iru"
        },
        {
          "from": "こゑ kowe 'voice'",
          "to": "こえ koe"
        },
        {
          "from": "をとこ wotoko 'man'",
          "to": "おとこ otoko",
          "note": "/w/ survives only before a; を is still written for the particle o."
